[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Type System Architecture and Primitive Types in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript extends JavaScript by adding static type definitions. Understanding its type system architecture and primitive types is fundamental for writing robust, scalable applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The TypeScript Type System Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike nominal type systems found in languages like Java or C#, TypeScript utilizes a structural type system. This means that type compatibility is determined by the member properties and methods, not by explicit declarations or inheritance hierarchies."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Erasure Model: Types exist purely at compile-time. During compilation, the TypeScript compiler strips away all interfaces and type annotations, leaving clean, standard JavaScript."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type Inference: TypeScript can automatically deduce types based on initial values, minimizing the need for explicit type annotations."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Primitive Types"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The foundation of the TypeScript type system consists of the JavaScript primitives, enhanced with static typing capabilities."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Explicitly typed primitives
let isPublished: boolean = true;
let articleId: number = 42;
let title: string = "TypeScript Architecture";

// Using template literal types
let statusMessage: `Status: ${string}` = "Status: Active";"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Special Types: Any, Unknown, Never, and Void"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript introduces several special types to handle edge cases and safety requirements in dynamic JavaScript environments."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"any: Opts out of type checking entirely. Use sparingly to avoid losing type safety."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"unknown: The type-safe counterpart to 'any'. You must perform type narrowing before operating on an unknown value."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"void: Represents the absence of a return value, commonly used in functions."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"never: Represents a state that should never occur, such as a function that always throws an error."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"function handleError(message: string): never {
    throw new Error(message);
}

function logMessage(msg: string): void {
    console.log(msg);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging structural typing and mastering primitive types alongside advanced utility types, developers can catch errors early in the development cycle and design maintainable software architectures."}]
